// Concrete at the mesoscale. Six convex polygonal aggregates (mat 3) --
// crushed stone, five to seven sides -- sit in mortar (mat 1), each wrapped
// in its interfacial transition zone (mat 2): the porous shell of cement
// paste that forms against every aggregate and is where concrete cracks
// first. The ITZ is 0.05 thick, and its outer face is the exact offset of the
// stone, straight along the sides and rounded about each corner. A seventh
// aggregate is cut by the specimen's left face, as a sawn face cuts it, so
// its ITZ ends on the boundary; two elliptical air voids sit in the mortar.
// Three-phase models of exactly this kind are the standard way to study how
// concrete fractures.
//
// What this domain is for. Thin shells round cornered inclusions. Each ITZ
// is an annulus 2.5 elements thick (NP = 100, h = 0.02 here) whose inner face
// has corners of 95 to 150 degrees and whose outer face has none, so a layout
// has to carry every corner of a stone out through a band too thin to hold a
// singularity -- seven times, at seven sizes and orientations. The stones'
// corners are no multiple of 90 degrees, so only each stone's own shape pins
// the singularities inside it. The mortar is one region with eight holes,
// six filled and two empty, and the cut aggregate adds four boundary
// junctions where a thin shell and a stone meet dS. There is no interior
// triple junction: the ITZ separates every other pair of materials.
//
// The stones are drawn from a seeded generator by rejection -- corners
// between 95 and 150 degrees, so that no vertex straddles BoundaryFeatures'
// 20-degree corner rule, and edges at least 0.1 long -- and placed with at
// least 4 elements of mortar between any two shells, a shell and a void, or a
// shell and the wall. Aggregate, ITZ and mortar are each one material, on 7,
// 7 and 1 surfaces.

// ---- generated geometry ----
